{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01YZdG8hCA6C5NmbmQ1FndpH","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tnarrative_guidance: 'Soften Claim 2 from ''fundamental blind spot'' to ''underappreciated\n2\t  methodological gap.'' Add explicit caveat on 38%: instability reflects mixture of\n3\t  capability improvement and DIF. Reframe ρ=0.997 as measurement efficiency problem.\n4\t  GSM1K downgraded to ''suggestive evidence.'' Position for ICLR findings track with\n5\t  rebuttal emphasizing semi-synthetic framework (Claim 3) as most portable contribution.'\n6\timagined_experiments:\n7\t- name: rl_robustness_validation\n8\t  plan_id: plan_002\n9\t  description: Validate DIF robustness to reasoning-RL models by adding 13 GRPO/PPO\n10\t    models to focal cohort and comparing DIF flag stability + traditional detector\n11\t    degradation.\n12\t  expected_results:\n13\t    n_reasoning_rl_models: 13\n14\t    n_alignment_rl_models: 477\n15\t    dif_flag_retention_ratio_sft_vs_alignment_rl: 0.91\n16\t    dif_flag_retention_ratio_sft_vs_reasoning_rl_added: 0.86\n17\t    dif_jaccard_sft_vs_rl_augmented: 0.83\n18\t    constat_auroc_sft_only: 0.72\n19\t    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.54\n20\t    mink_auroc_sft_only: 0.68\n21\t    mink_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.49\n22\t    delong_dif_vs_constat_p: 0.087\n23\t    delong_dif_vs_mink_p: 0.042\n24\t    tost_equivalence_dif_retention_p: 0.11\n25\t    bootstrap_ci_dif_retention:\n26\t    - 0.79\n27\t    - 0.93\n28\t    power_curve_detection_boundary_p: 0.55\n29\t  result_rationale: 'With only N=13 reasoning-RL models, group-level DIF remains stable\n30\t    (retention 0.86) because DIF operates on full cohort N≥80 and RL models are a\n31\t    minority. Traditional detectors degrade individually (ConStat AUROC 0.72→0.54,\n32\t    Min-K% 0.68→0.49) as expected from NLL gap contraction. However, DeLong test for\n33\t    DIF vs ConStat is marginal (p=0.087) — underpowered at N=13. TOST equivalence\n34\t    fails (p=0.11 > 0.05), so reviewers can''t rule out that DIF retention is coincidental.\n35\t    Median-tier outcome: suggestive robustness evidence insufficient for strong claim.\n36\t    Reviewers note ''N=13 is too few for definitive comparison; interesting pilot\n37\t    but not convincing.'' Detection boundary at p=0.55 means DIF misses partial exploitation\n38\t    below 55%, which is a meaningful gap.'\n39\t  tables:\n40\t  - title: 'Table: DIF vs Traditional Detector Comparison Under RL'\n41\t    columns:\n42\t    - Detector\n43\t    - AUROC (SFT)\n44\t    - AUROC (Alignment-RL)\n45\t    - AUROC (Reasoning-RL)\n46\t    - Δ\n47\t    - 95% CI\n48\t    rows:\n49\t    - - DIF (MH)\n50\t      - N/A (group)\n51\t      - N/A (group)\n52\t      - retention=0.86\n53\t      - '-0.14'\n54\t      - '[−0.21, −0.07]'\n55\t    - - ConStat\n56\t      - '0.72'\n57\t      - '0.69'\n58\t      - '0.54'\n59\t      - '-0.18'\n60\t      - '[−0.31, −0.05]'\n61\t    - - Min-K%\n62\t      - '0.68'\n63\t      - '0.64'\n64\t      - '0.49'\n65\t      - '-0.19'\n66\t      - '[−0.33, −0.06]'\n67\t  figures_description:\n68\t  - 'Figure: Detection power as function of exploitation rate p ∈ [0.4, 1.0]. DIF\n69\t    power curve shows detection boundary at p≈0.55 (TPR>0.5). Below p=0.55, DIF effect\n70\t    size shrinks below δ=1.4 threshold. Shaded region shows ensemble coverage handoff\n71\t    zone (p=0.4-0.6) where ConStat on non-RL models still operates.'\n72\t  - 'Figure: Jaccard similarity of DIF flag sets across cohort compositions (SFT-only,\n73\t    +alignment-RL, +reasoning-RL). Box plots over 100 bootstrap resamples showing\n74\t    overlap stability.'\n75\t  analysis: Result is directionally supportive but statistically underpowered. DIF\n76\t    shows robustness (86% flag retention) while traditional detectors degrade, consistent\n77\t    with theoretical prediction. However, TOST equivalence failure and marginal DeLong\n78\t    test mean this cannot be claimed as definitive proof. In median tier, reviewers\n79\t    acknowledge the logic but request either (a) more reasoning-RL models when available,\n80\t    or (b) formal power analysis showing what N would be needed for conclusive evidence.\n81\t    The detection boundary at p=0.55 is an honest limitation — partial exploitation\n82\t    remains a blind spot. Best positioned as 'preliminary evidence consistent with\n83\t    theoretical robustness prediction' in limitations/future work, not as a standalone\n84\t    claim.\n85\t- name: constat_perturbation_eval\n86\t  plan_id: plan_003a\n87\t  description: Independent ConStat-style perturbation testing on 250 MMLU items with\n88\t    LLM-generated semantic reformulations across 15 models.\n89\t  expected_results:\n90\t    n_items_reformulated: 248\n91\t    n_models_tested: 15\n92\t    mean_performance_drop_original_vs_reformulated: 0.034\n93\t    constat_detection_rate_fdr_010: 0.127\n94\t    constat_detection_rate_fdr_005: 0.089\n95\t    constat_auroc_vs_li_labels: 0.61\n96\t    constat_auroc_non_rl_models: 0.67\n97\t    constat_auroc_rl_models: 0.52\n98\t    mean_reformulation_quality_score: 0.82\n99\t    semantic_equivalence_human_check: 0.91\n100\t    effect_size_detected_items_cohens_d: 0.73\n101\t  result_rationale: ConStat operates at per-model level detecting performance drops\n102\t    on reformulations. On non-RL models (where memorization manifests as sensitivity\n103\t    to surface form), detection rate is moderate (12.7% at FDR=0.10). RL models show\n104\t    minimal surface-form sensitivity (AUROC 0.52, near chance) because PPO/GRPO training\n105\t    generalizes beyond verbatim patterns. The 3.4% mean performance drop across all\n106\t    items is small because most items aren't contaminated — only a subset shows the\n107\t    characteristic bimodal response pattern. AUROC vs Li et al. labels is weak (0.61)\n108\t    because, as established, web-overlap is a mismatched ground truth for item-level\n109\t    detection. In median tier, this provides a useful baseline comparison confirming\n110\t    ConStat's complementary detection space (non-RL, surface-form memorization) but\n111\t    doesn't change the paper's core narrative.\n112\t  tables:\n113\t  - title: 'Table: ConStat Detection Rates by Model Category'\n114\t    columns:\n115\t    - Model Category\n116\t    - N Models\n117\t    - Detection Rate (FDR=0.10)\n118\t    - Mean Effect Size (d)\n119\t    rows:\n120\t    - - SFT-only\n121\t      - '6'\n122\t      - '0.153'\n123\t      - '0.81'\n124\t    - - Alignment-RL (DPO)\n125\t      - '5'\n126\t      - '0.134'\n127\t      - '0.69'\n128\t    - - Reasoning-RL (GRPO)\n129\t      - '4'\n130\t      - '0.071'\n131\t      - '0.42'\n132\t  figures_description:\n133\t  - 'Figure: Distribution of per-item performance drops (original − reformulated)\n134\t    across 248 items. Bimodal distribution visible: bulk of items near zero, contaminated\n135\t    tail at Δ>0.15.'\n136\t  analysis: ConStat independently validates the complementary detection space claim\n137\t    — it works where DIF doesn't (per-model, small effects) but fails where DIF succeeds\n138\t    (RL-robust, group-differential). However, in median tier this experiment is not\n139\t    included in the main paper because plan_003c (ensemble) was never fully executed\n140\t    and the ensemble narrative (original Claim 2) was dropped during the pivot. ConStat\n141\t    results go to appendix as supplementary comparison, supporting the 'detection\n142\t    methods are complementary' theme without being a core contribution.\n143\t- name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n144\t  plan_id: plan_003b\n145\t  description: DVD generation variance detection on 12 open-weight models with 50\n146\t    generations per item at T=0.8 on 300 MMLU items.\n147\t  expected_results:\n148\t    n_models_tested: 12\n149\t    n_items_per_model: 300\n150\t    n_generations_per_item: 50\n151\t    temperature: 0.8\n152\t    bimodal_detection_rate: 0.094\n153\t    dvd_auroc_vs_dif_flags: 0.58\n154\t    dvd_auroc_verbatim_contamination: 0.71\n155\t    dvd_auroc_variant_contamination: 0.63\n156\t    mean_generation_variance_clean_items: 0.34\n157\t    mean_generation_variance_contaminated_items: 0.18\n158\t    gpu_hours_used: 62\n159\t    inference_failures: 3\n160\t  result_rationale: 'DVD detects contamination via low generation variance (memorized\n161\t    items → consistently correct). Detection rate of 9.4% reflects that most contamination\n162\t    on MMLU is not verbatim memorization but pattern-level familiarity, which still\n163\t    produces moderate variance. AUROC for verbatim contamination (0.71) is better\n164\t    than variant (0.63) because verbatim produces clearer bimodal distributions. Correlation\n165\t    with DIF flags is weak (AUROC 0.58) because DIF and DVD measure fundamentally\n166\t    different signals: DIF measures cross-group differential, DVD measures within-model\n167\t    consistency. In median tier, DVD provides another complementary signal but is\n168\t    expensive (62 GPU-hours for 12 models × 300 items) and the low detection rate\n169\t    limits practical utility. The 3 inference failures (OOM on larger models) highlight\n170\t    scalability concerns.'\n171\t  tables:\n172\t  - title: 'Table: DVD Detection Characteristics'\n173\t    columns:\n174\t    - Contamination Type\n175\t    - AUROC\n176\t    - Detection Rate (threshold=0.10)\n177\t    - Mean Variance Reduction\n178\t    rows:\n179\t    - - Verbatim\n180\t      - '0.71'\n181\t      - '0.134'\n182\t      - '0.21'\n183\t    - - Variant/Paraphrase\n184\t      - '0.63'\n185\t      - '0.072'\n186\t      - '0.12'\n187\t    - - None (clean)\n188\t      - —\n189\t      - 0.031 (FPR)\n190\t      - —\n191\t  figures_description:\n192\t  - 'Figure: Generation variance distributions for DIF-flagged vs DIF-stable items\n193\t    across 12 models. Modest leftward shift for DIF-flagged items but heavy overlap,\n194\t    confirming weak correspondence between group-differential (DIF) and within-model\n195\t    (DVD) signals.'\n196\t  analysis: DVD results are informative but not compelling enough for inclusion in\n197\t    the pivoted paper. The weak correspondence with DIF flags (AUROC 0.58) actually\n198\t    supports the paper's narrative that different detection methods capture different\n199\t    contamination mechanisms. However, the high GPU cost and low detection rates make\n200\t    this a poor cost-benefit trade for the characterization paper. In median tier,\n201\t    this remains in supplementary material at best. The result is useful for future\n202\t    ensemble work but doesn't strengthen any of the four core claims.\n203\t- name: ensemble_fusion\n204\t  plan_id: plan_003c\n205\t  description: Attempt to fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble with\n206\t    majority voting and weighted fusion.\n207\t  expected_results:\n208\t    ensemble_2of3_precision: 0.41\n209\t    ensemble_2of3_recall: 0.23\n210\t    ensemble_weighted_auroc: 0.66\n211\t    ensemble_fpr: 0.087\n212\t    individual_dif_auroc: 0.62\n213\t    individual_constat_auroc: 0.61\n214\t    individual_dvd_auroc: 0.58\n215\t    cross_val_weight_variance: 0.15\n216\t    uniform_vs_optimized_auroc_gap: 0.03\n217\t    coverage_overlap_dif_constat: 0.31\n218\t    coverage_overlap_dif_dvd: 0.22\n219\t    coverage_overlap_constat_dvd: 0.38\n220\t  result_rationale: Ensemble shows modest improvement over individuals (AUROC 0.66\n221\t    vs best individual 0.62) but the improvement is not dramatic because the three\n222\t    signals have limited overlap — they detect different contamination mechanisms\n223\t    with low redundancy. 2-of-3 majority voting has good precision (0.41) but very\n224\t    low recall (0.23) because few items trigger multiple detectors simultaneously.\n225\t    FPR<0.10 criterion is met (0.087) but only because the ensemble is very conservative.\n226\t    Cross-validation weight variance is high (0.15) indicating instability — different\n227\t    folds produce different optimal weights, suggesting the calibration set is too\n228\t    small for reliable fusion. In median tier, this experiment is effectively abandoned\n229\t    during the pivot away from 'detection methods' framing. The ensemble was the core\n230\t    of original Claim 2 which is now dropped.\n231\t  tables:\n232\t  - title: 'Table: Ensemble vs Individual Detector Performance'\n233\t    columns:\n234\t    - Method\n235\t    - AUROC\n236\t    - Precision@FPR=0.10\n237\t    - Coverage (items flagged)\n238\t    rows:\n239\t    - - DIF (MH)\n240\t      - '0.62'\n241\t      - '0.38'\n242\t      - 31.3%\n243\t    - - ConStat\n244\t      - '0.61'\n245\t      - '0.34'\n246\t      - 12.7%\n247\t    - - DVD\n248\t      - '0.58'\n249\t      - '0.29'\n250\t      - 9.4%\n251\t    - - Ensemble (2-of-3)\n252\t      - '0.66'\n253\t      - '0.41'\n254\t      - 6.8%\n255\t    - - Ensemble (weighted)\n256\t      - '0.66'\n257\t      - '0.43'\n258\t      - 8.2%\n259\t  figures_description:\n260\t  - 'Figure: Venn diagram showing overlap of flagged items across three detectors.\n261\t    DIF flags ~3,900 items, ConStat ~380, DVD ~280. Triple overlap: 47 items. Confirms\n262\t    complementary rather than redundant coverage.'\n263\t  analysis: 'Ensemble fusion fails to produce a compelling unified detector because\n264\t    the three methods target different contamination mechanisms with minimal overlap.\n265\t    This is actually the correct scientific conclusion — it supports the pivot away\n266\t    from ''unified contamination detector'' toward ''characterization of differential\n267\t    item functioning.'' In median tier, plan_003c is reported as a negative result\n268\t    in the appendix: ''ensemble approaches require contamination mechanisms to be\n269\t    partially redundant; when mechanisms are orthogonal, fusion provides minimal lift.''\n270\t    This negative result paradoxically supports the paper''s reframing: DIF measures\n271\t    something fundamentally different from per-model detectors.'\n272\t- name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n273\t  plan_id: plan_005\n274\t  description: Fit bifactor IRT model (general + 57 subject-specific factors) to METABENCH\n275\t    MMLU response matrix and attempt test equating via anchor items.\n276\t  expected_results:\n277\t    bifactor_converged: true\n278\t    n_iterations_to_converge: 847\n279\t    general_factor_variance_explained: 0.62\n280\t    mean_subject_factor_variance: 0.14\n281\t    rmsea_bifactor: 0.041\n282\t    cfi_bifactor: 0.87\n283\t    n_anchor_items_selected: 1842\n284\t    anchor_stability_criterion: DIF A-class across all cohort pairs\n285\t    equated_rho_original_vs_replaced: 0.93\n286\t    equated_rho_anchor_only: 0.97\n287\t    replacement_item_calibration_rmse: 0.38\n288\t    kim_kolen_fixed_param_fit: acceptable for 68% of replacement items\n289\t  result_rationale: Bifactor model converges but with marginal fit (CFI=0.87 < 0.90\n290\t    threshold, RMSEA=0.041 acceptable). The 57-subject structure is too fine-grained\n291\t    for the model to fit cleanly — general factor explains 62% of variance, individual\n292\t    subjects contribute 14% on average, leaving ~24% residual. Anchor item selection\n293\t    yields 1,842 stable items (14.7% of total), sufficient for equating but a relatively\n294\t    small anchor set. Equated score correlation (ρ=0.93) falls below the target ρ≥0.95,\n295\t    primarily because replacement item calibration via fixed-parameter approach has\n296\t    high RMSE (0.38) — the item parameters estimated on one cohort don't transfer\n297\t    perfectly. In median tier, this is a 'proof of concept with caveats' — equating\n298\t    is feasible in principle but the bifactor model's marginal fit and the below-target\n299\t    equated correlation weaken the regeneration claim. Reviewers note 'interesting\n300\t    pilot but ρ=0.93 suggests non-trivial information loss during equating.'\n301\t  tables:\n302\t  - title: 'Table: Bifactor Model Fit Indices'\n303\t    columns:\n304\t    - Index\n305\t    - Value\n306\t    - Acceptable\n307\t    - Good\n308\t    rows:\n309\t    - - RMSEA\n310\t      - '0.041'\n311\t      - < 0.08 ✓\n312\t      - < 0.05 ✓\n313\t    - - CFI\n314\t      - '0.87'\n315\t      - < 0.90 ✗\n316\t      - ≥ 0.95 ✗\n317\t    - - TLI\n318\t      - '0.84'\n319\t      - < 0.90 ✗\n320\t      - ≥ 0.95 ✗\n321\t    - - SRMR\n322\t      - '0.063'\n323\t      - < 0.08 ✓\n324\t      - < 0.05 ✗\n325\t  figures_description:\n326\t  - 'Figure: Scree plot showing general factor dominant (eigenvalue=3,847) followed\n327\t    by 57 subject-specific factors with eigenvalues 12-180. Clear elbow after general\n328\t    factor confirms unidimensional dominance with multidimensional residual structure.'\n329\t  - 'Figure: Scatter plot of equated vs original model scores (N=5,219). Correlation\n330\t    ρ=0.93, with visible heteroscedasticity — low-ability models show more equating\n331\t    error than high-ability models.'\n332\t  analysis: Bifactor equating is technically feasible but falls short of production\n333\t    quality. The marginal model fit (CFI=0.87) reflects the known challenge that 57\n334\t    MMLU subjects create a high-dimensional latent space poorly captured by a single\n335\t    general factor. In median tier, this experiment is reported honestly as 'pilot\n336\t    demonstration with limitations' — it shows the path to benchmark regeneration\n337\t    exists but requires better IRT models (multidimensional, or domain-clustered bifactor).\n338\t    Reviewers would likely say 'interesting future direction but not mature enough\n339\t    for a contribution claim.' This supports the paper's positioning of equating as\n340\t    future work rather than a current claim. Plan_014 already showed within-subject\n341\t    C%=38.1%, so the bifactor pre-check approach for reducing DIF was killed — this\n342\t    equating pilot inherits that limitation.\n343\t- name: item_generation_calibration\n344\t  plan_id: plan_006\n345\t  description: Generate replacement items for 200 DIF-flagged MMLU questions using\n346\t    LLM generation with construct constraints, calibrate via student simulation.\n347\t  expected_results:\n348\t    n_items_generated: 200\n349\t    n_items_passing_quality_filter: 156\n350\t    difficulty_correlation_prompt_based: 0.31\n351\t    difficulty_correlation_student_sim: 0.64\n352\t    discrimination_correlation: 0.42\n353\t    construct_alignment_human_rating: 0.78\n354\t    holdout_panel_correlation: 0.58\n355\t    mean_difficulty_mae: 0.18\n356\t    pct_items_within_05_difficulty: 0.43\n357\t    gpu_hours_used: 187\n358\t  result_rationale: Item generation produces grammatically correct, construct-aligned\n359\t    questions (78% human-rated alignment) but calibration accuracy is moderate. Student\n360\t    simulation improves difficulty prediction (ρ=0.64) over prompt-based targeting\n361\t    (ρ=0.31), consistent with literature (arXiv 2601.09953 reports r=0.75-0.82 — our\n362\t    lower value reflects MMLU's broader domain coverage vs focused benchmarks). Only\n363\t    43% of generated items fall within ±0.5 difficulty of the target, meaning majority\n364\t    would need iterative recalibration. Discrimination correlation is lower (0.42)\n365\t    because item discrimination depends on subtle psychometric properties harder to\n366\t    control via generation. In median tier, this demonstrates that LLM item generation\n367\t    is a viable but immature pipeline — insufficient for automated benchmark regeneration\n368\t    without human-in-the-loop validation. Reviewers would flag the 58% holdout correlation\n369\t    as 'items don't transfer well to unseen models.'\n370\t  tables:\n371\t  - title: 'Table: Calibration Method Comparison'\n372\t    columns:\n373\t    - Method\n374\t    - Difficulty ρ\n375\t    - Discrimination ρ\n376\t    - '% Within ±0.5 Target'\n377\t    rows:\n378\t    - - Prompt-based targeting\n379\t      - '0.31'\n380\t      - '0.28'\n381\t      - '0.24'\n382\t    - - Student simulation (5 students)\n383\t      - '0.64'\n384\t      - '0.42'\n385\t      - '0.43'\n386\t    - - Student simulation (20 students)\n387\t      - '0.68'\n388\t      - '0.45'\n389\t      - '0.47'\n390\t  figures_description:\n391\t  - 'Figure: Predicted vs actual item difficulty for 156 generated items. Student\n392\t    simulation shows tighter scatter (ρ=0.64) vs prompt-based (ρ=0.31), but both show\n393\t    ceiling/floor compression — hardest and easiest items are poorly calibrated.'\n394\t  analysis: 'Item generation calibration shows promise but not maturity. The gap between\n395\t    student simulation performance here (ρ=0.64) and published results (r=0.75-0.82)\n396\t    likely reflects MMLU''s domain breadth — calibrating ''abstract algebra'' items\n397\t    requires different student models than ''clinical knowledge.'' In median tier,\n398\t    this supports the future work framing: ''automated benchmark regeneration is feasible\n399\t    with improved calibration pipelines'' but is not ready as a standalone contribution.\n400\t    Combined with plan_005''s marginal equating results, the regeneration storyline\n401\t    (original Claim 3) remains firmly in future work territory. The 187 GPU-hours\n402\t    for 200 items also highlights cost concerns for full-scale deployment.'\n403\t- name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n404\t  plan_id: plan_007\n405\t  description: Simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF with informative priors from prior\n406\t    administrations over 4 successive benchmark waves.\n407\t  expected_results:\n408\t    single_administration_power_delta10: 0.41\n409\t    cumulative_2_administrations_power: 0.54\n410\t    cumulative_3_administrations_power: 0.67\n411\t    cumulative_4_administrations_power: 0.74\n412\t    single_administration_fpr: 0.042\n413\t    cumulative_4_fpr: 0.051\n414\t    power_gain_per_administration:\n415\t    - 0.13\n416\t    - 0.13\n417\t    - 0.07\n418\t    convergence_plateau: ~4 administrations\n419\t    bayes_factor_threshold: 3.0\n420\t    pct_items_bf_gt_3_after_4_admin: 0.68\n421\t  result_rationale: Cumulative Bayesian approach shows expected power improvement\n422\t    (0.41→0.74 over 4 administrations for medium effects δ=1.0) as evidence accumulates.\n423\t    This is a well-established psychometric result (Sinharay 2006) applied to the\n424\t    LLM benchmark setting. FPR remains controlled (0.051) because Bayesian shrinkage\n425\t    prevents false alarms from accumulating. Power gains show diminishing returns\n426\t    (0.13, 0.13, 0.07) — the fourth administration adds less because most detectable\n427\t    items are already identified. In median tier, this is simulation-only (no real\n428\t    longitudinal MMLU data exists with 4 temporal waves), so reviewers note 'theoretically\n429\t    sound but purely simulated — would need actual longitudinal benchmark deployment\n430\t    to validate.' Positioned as future monitoring methodology, not a current empirical\n431\t    contribution.\n432\t  tables:\n433\t  - title: 'Table: Cumulative Power by Effect Size'\n434\t    columns:\n435\t    - Administrations\n436\t    - δ=0.8\n437\t    - δ=1.0\n438\t    - δ=1.2\n439\t    - δ=1.4\n440\t    rows:\n441\t    - - '1'\n442\t      - '0.24'\n443\t      - '0.41'\n444\t      - '0.55'\n445\t      - '0.68'\n446\t    - - '2'\n447\t      - '0.37'\n448\t      - '0.54'\n449\t      - '0.69'\n450\t      - '0.81'\n451\t    - - '3'\n452\t      - '0.48'\n453\t      - '0.67'\n454\t      - '0.79'\n455\t      - '0.89'\n456\t    - - '4'\n457\t      - '0.54'\n458\t      - '0.74'\n459\t      - '0.84'\n460\t      - '0.92'\n461\t  figures_description:\n462\t  - 'Figure: Power accumulation curves across 4 administrations, stratified by effect\n463\t    size. Clear sigmoid shape with plateau around administration 3-4. Dashed line\n464\t    at TPR=0.50 shows that medium effects (δ=1.0) become reliably detectable only\n465\t    after 2+ administrations.'\n466\t  analysis: 'Bayesian cumulative DIF is theoretically elegant and directly addresses\n467\t    the power limitation identified in plan_001 (single-shot DIF only detects large\n468\t    effects δ≥1.4). However, it''s purely simulation-based in the current paper because\n469\t    METABENCH provides only a single temporal snapshot (2023 vs 2024-H1), not 4 successive\n470\t    administrations. In median tier, reviewers appreciate the methodological contribution\n471\t    but note ''this requires longitudinal benchmark infrastructure that doesn''t yet\n472\t    exist.'' Best positioned as a concrete recommendation for benchmark maintainers:\n473\t    ''if you collect response matrices over time, here''s how to accumulate evidence\n474\t    of item degradation.'' Strengthens the ''measurement science for ML benchmarks''\n475\t    narrative without requiring new empirical data.'\n476\t- name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n477\t  plan_id: plan_008\n478\t  description: Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement\n479\t    (5% to 50%) using METABENCH empirical data.\n480\t  expected_results:\n481\t    rho_5pct_replacement: 0.998\n482\t    rho_10pct_replacement: 0.996\n483\t    rho_20pct_replacement: 0.991\n484\t    rho_50pct_replacement: 0.972\n485\t    reliability_alpha_full: 0.943\n486\t    reliability_alpha_50pct_replaced: 0.921\n487\t    construct_coverage_5pct: 0.97\n488\t    construct_coverage_50pct: 0.84\n489\t    theoretical_bound_rho_vs_replacement_rate: ρ ≥ 1 - 1.3r (r = replacement fraction)\n490\t    critical_replacement_threshold: ~35% before ρ < 0.98\n491\t    dif_targeted_vs_random_replacement_rho_gap: 0.008\n492\t  result_rationale: 'Progressive replacement analysis reveals extreme ranking robustness\n493\t    — even 50% item replacement maintains ρ=0.972. This is consistent with plan_017''s\n494\t    finding that ρ=0.997 after removing 31% DIF items. MMLU''s 12,508 items provide\n495\t    massive redundancy; any subset of ~6,000+ items preserves relative model ordering.\n496\t    Reliability (Cronbach''s α) degrades more noticeably (0.943→0.921 at 50%) because\n497\t    replacement items have higher calibration error. Construct coverage (proportion\n498\t    of original 57 subjects adequately sampled) drops to 0.84 at 50% because some\n499\t    small subjects (N<50 items) lose coverage entirely. The near-linear relationship\n500\t    ρ ≥ 1 - 1.3r holds empirically up to r=0.4. In median tier, this result cuts both\n501\t    ways: it shows equating is feasible (replacements don''t destroy validity) but\n502\t    also undermines urgency (if rankings are so stable, why bother replacing items?).\n503\t    Reviewers invoke the ''so what'' critique: ρ=0.997→0.972 is still excellent.'\n504\t  tables:\n505\t  - title: 'Table: Validity Metrics Under Progressive Replacement'\n506\t    columns:\n507\t    - Replacement %\n508\t    - Spearman ρ\n509\t    - Cronbach α\n510\t    - Construct Coverage\n511\t    - Max Rank Δ\n512\t    rows:\n513\t    - - 0% (baseline)\n514\t      - '1.000'\n515\t      - '0.943'\n516\t      - '1.00'\n517\t      - '0'\n518\t    - - 5%\n519\t      - '0.998'\n520\t      - '0.941'\n521\t      - '0.97'\n522\t      - '23'\n523\t    - - 10%\n524\t      - '0.996'\n525\t      - '0.938'\n526\t      - '0.95'\n527\t      - '47'\n528\t    - - 20%\n529\t      - '0.991'\n530\t      - '0.932'\n531\t      - '0.91'\n532\t      - '112'\n533\t    - - 50%\n534\t      - '0.972'\n535\t      - '0.921'\n536\t      - '0.84'\n537\t      - '341'\n538\t  figures_description:\n539\t  - 'Figure: Ranking stability (ρ) vs replacement fraction. Near-linear degradation\n540\t    with empirical bound overlaid. Shaded confidence band from 100 bootstrap resamples.\n541\t    Vertical dashed line at 31% (observed DIF percentage) showing ρ=0.993.'\n542\t  analysis: 'Graceful degradation analysis primarily serves as supplementary validation\n543\t    for plan_017''s core finding. It formalizes what the ρ=0.997 result already implies:\n544\t    MMLU has enormous measurement redundancy. In median tier, this becomes a double-edged\n545\t    sword — reviewers use it to argue ''if the benchmark is so redundant, the 38%\n546\t    instability finding is merely academic, not practically concerning.'' The paper''s\n547\t    rebuttal must be: ''redundancy protects rankings but masks that 31% of items carry\n548\t    disproportionate discriminative information (z=-8 to -49σ from plan_017). Efficiency,\n549\t    not validity, is the real concern.'' The 35% critical threshold aligns with the\n550\t    observed 38% DIF rate — we''re near the edge where replacement starts to matter,\n551\t    even if we haven''t crossed it yet.'\n552\t- name: item_focused_trees\n553\t  plan_id: plan_009\n554\t  description: Implement Item-Focused Trees treating model release date as continuous\n555\t    covariate, comparing detection power and flexibility vs categorical temporal DIF.\n556\t  expected_results:\n557\t    n_items_analyzed: 12508\n558\t    ift_c_pct_equivalent: 28.4\n559\t    ift_vs_categorical_jaccard: 0.72\n560\t    ift_unique_flags: 0.08\n561\t    categorical_unique_flags: 0.12\n562\t    ift_split_point_median: '2023-09-15'\n563\t    ift_nonlinear_items_pct: 0.14\n564\t    ift_power_gain_vs_categorical: 0.03\n565\t    computation_time_hours: 4.2\n566\t  result_rationale: Item-Focused Trees detect 28.4% of items as exhibiting DIF along\n567\t    the release-date continuum (vs 31.3% for categorical temporal split). Jaccard\n568\t    overlap of 0.72 with categorical DIF shows substantial agreement — most items\n569\t    flagged by IFT are also flagged by categorical MH, confirming the temporal split\n570\t    was reasonable. IFT identifies 8% unique items (gradual drift not captured by\n571\t    binary split) and misses 12% that categorical catches (items with sharp discontinuity\n572\t    better detected by group contrast). 14% of items show nonlinear DIF trajectories\n573\t    (early models and late models both struggle, middle cohort performs differently).\n574\t    Power gain is minimal (+0.03) because the binary temporal split already captures\n575\t    the primary contrast. In median tier, IFT provides methodological sophistication\n576\t    but minimal empirical gain — reviewers appreciate the robustness check ('arbitrary\n577\t    temporal split didn't distort results') but note 'the continuous approach doesn't\n578\t    change your main finding.'\n579\t  tables:\n580\t  - title: 'Table: IFT vs Categorical DIF Comparison'\n581\t    columns:\n582\t    - Method\n583\t    - Items Flagged\n584\t    - Unique Flags\n585\t    - Overlap (Jaccard)\n586\t    - Computation Time\n587\t    rows:\n588\t    - - Categorical MH (2023 vs 2024-H1)\n589\t      - 31.3%\n590\t      - 12%\n591\t      - —\n592\t      - 0.3 hours\n593\t    - - Item-Focused Trees (continuous date)\n594\t      - 28.4%\n595\t      - 8%\n596\t      - '0.72'\n597\t      - 4.2 hours\n598\t  figures_description:\n599\t  - 'Figure: Example IFT splits for 4 items showing different DIF trajectories. (a)\n600\t    Sharp discontinuity at 2023-11 (well-detected by categorical), (b) Gradual linear\n601\t    drift (better detected by IFT), (c) U-shaped nonlinear pattern (only detected\n602\t    by IFT), (d) No DIF (flat).'\n603\t  analysis: 'IFT serves primarily as a robustness check confirming that the categorical\n604\t    temporal split was not misleading. The 72% overlap validates the simpler approach\n605\t    while the 14% nonlinear items are methodologically interesting but don''t change\n606\t    conclusions. In median tier, this goes to supplementary material as ''sensitivity\n607\t    analysis: continuous treatment of release date produces concordant results (Jaccard=0.72),\n608\t    with 14% of items showing nonlinear trajectories warranting future investigation.''\n609\t    Reviewers may note that IFT is more principled but the limited power gain doesn''t\n610\t    justify the complexity for the main paper. Most useful as a reviewer rebuttal\n611\t    resource: ''our arbitrary temporal split is validated by the continuous approach.'''\n612\t- name: architecture_stratified_dif\n613\t  plan_id: plan_010\n614\t  description: Run DIF within decoder-only architecture subpopulation to test whether\n615\t    IRT fit improves and spurious DIF reduces when architecture heterogeneity is removed.\n616\t  expected_results:\n617\t    n_decoder_only_models: 3412\n618\t    n_ref_decoder_only_2023: 891\n619\t    n_foc_decoder_only_2024h1: 523\n620\t    decoder_only_c_pct: 28.2\n621\t    full_population_c_pct: 31.3\n622\t    delta_c_pct: -3.1\n623\t    rmsea_full_population: 0.089\n624\t    rmsea_decoder_only: 0.067\n625\t    cfi_full_population: 0.78\n626\t    cfi_decoder_only: 0.83\n627\t    tli_decoder_only: 0.81\n628\t    srmr_decoder_only: 0.058\n629\t    jaccard_decoder_vs_full: 0.81\n630\t    fpr_decoder_only: 0.282\n631\t    tpr_decoder_only_semi_synthetic_p05: 0.79\n632\t    delta_tpr_decoder_only: 0.508\n633\t  result_rationale: 'Architecture stratification reduces C% from 31.3% to 28.2% —\n634\t    a modest 3.1pp improvement, not the dramatic reduction that would indicate architecture\n635\t    heterogeneity was the primary driver of spurious DIF. IRT fit improves marginally\n636\t    (RMSEA 0.089→0.067, from ''poor'' to ''marginal''; CFI 0.78→0.83, still below\n637\t    0.90 ''acceptable''). This confirms that architecture mixing contributes some\n638\t    model misfit but is not the dominant source — the remaining 28.2% DIF reflects\n639\t    genuine temporal item instability within decoder-only models. Jaccard overlap\n640\t    of 0.81 with full-population flags confirms most DIF items are flagged regardless\n641\t    of population composition. In median tier, this is the ''modest improvement''\n642\t    outcome specified: reviewers acknowledge architecture stratification helps but\n643\t    note ''RMSEA=0.067 and CFI=0.83 are still below standard fit thresholds, suggesting\n644\t    deeper structural issues with unidimensional IRT on MMLU.'''\n645\t  tables:\n646\t  - title: 'Table: IRT Fit Comparison: Full Population vs Decoder-Only'\n647\t    columns:\n648\t    - Metric\n649\t    - Full Population\n650\t    - Decoder-Only\n651\t    - Improvement\n652\t    - Acceptable Threshold\n653\t    rows:\n654\t    - - RMSEA\n655\t      - '0.089'\n656\t      - '0.067'\n657\t      - −0.022\n658\t      - < 0.05\n659\t    - - CFI\n660\t      - '0.78'\n661\t      - '0.83'\n662\t      - '+0.05'\n663\t      - ≥ 0.90\n664\t    - - TLI\n665\t      - '0.75'\n666\t      - '0.81'\n667\t      - '+0.06'\n668\t      - ≥ 0.90\n669\t    - - SRMR\n670\t      - '0.074'\n671\t      - '0.058'\n672\t      - −0.016\n673\t      - < 0.05\n674\t    - - C% (ETS)\n675\t      - 31.3%\n676\t      - 28.2%\n677\t      - −3.1pp\n678\t      - —\n679\t  figures_description:\n680\t  - 'Figure: Item-level DIF effect sizes (|Δ_MH|) for decoder-only vs full population.\n681\t    Scatter plot showing high correlation (r=0.91) with slight downward shift for\n682\t    decoder-only, confirming most DIF persists within homogeneous architecture.'\n683\t  - 'Figure: RMSEA distribution across 57 MMLU subjects for decoder-only population.\n684\t    Most subjects in 0.05-0.08 range (marginal fit), with moral_scenarios and professional_law\n685\t    as outliers (>0.10).'\n686\t  analysis: 'Plan_010 is the proactive defense experiment against the ''IRT model\n687\t    misfit'' reviewer concern. In median tier, the result is honest but not fully\n688\t    reassuring: architecture stratification helps (3.1pp C% reduction, RMSEA improvement)\n689\t    but doesn''t solve the fundamental issue that unidimensional IRT is a simplification\n690\t    for 57-subject MMLU. The paper frames this as: ''architecture stratification provides\n691\t    modest improvement, confirming that population homogeneity is beneficial but not\n692\t    sufficient — multidimensional IRT or domain-specific modeling remain future directions.''\n693\t    Reviewers likely say ''you''ve shown the problem is partially addressable but\n694\t    haven''t solved it — reframe 38% as upper bound, 28% as lower bound under controlled\n695\t    conditions.'' This is consistent with median-tier narrative guidance: ''instability\n696\t    reflects a mixture'' with explicit bounds.'\n697\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80d446f8-6835-4587-bd13-fde4c02ffb42","uuid":"9121a898-d94a-4dac-9e90-331c5d17b51e","timestamp":"2026-05-21T02:06:27.088Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T012912/median/review_context.yaml","content":"narrative_guidance: 'Soften Claim 2 from ''fundamental blind spot'' to ''underappreciated\n  methodological gap.'' Add explicit caveat on 38%: instability reflects mixture of\n  capability improvement and DIF. Reframe ρ=0.997 as measurement efficiency problem.\n  GSM1K downgraded to ''suggestive evidence.'' Position for ICLR findings track with\n  rebuttal emphasizing semi-synthetic framework (Claim 3) as most portable contribution.'\nimagined_experiments:\n- name: rl_robustness_validation\n  plan_id: plan_002\n  description: Validate DIF robustness to reasoning-RL models by adding 13 GRPO/PPO\n    models to focal cohort and comparing DIF flag stability + traditional detector\n    degradation.\n  expected_results:\n    n_reasoning_rl_models: 13\n    n_alignment_rl_models: 477\n    dif_flag_retention_ratio_sft_vs_alignment_rl: 0.91\n    dif_flag_retention_ratio_sft_vs_reasoning_rl_added: 0.86\n    dif_jaccard_sft_vs_rl_augmented: 0.83\n    constat_auroc_sft_only: 0.72\n    constat_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.54\n    mink_auroc_sft_only: 0.68\n    mink_auroc_reasoning_rl: 0.49\n    delong_dif_vs_constat_p: 0.087\n    delong_dif_vs_mink_p: 0.042\n    tost_equivalence_dif_retention_p: 0.11\n    bootstrap_ci_dif_retention:\n    - 0.79\n    - 0.93\n    power_curve_detection_boundary_p: 0.55\n  result_rationale: 'With only N=13 reasoning-RL models, group-level DIF remains stable\n    (retention 0.86) because DIF operates on full cohort N≥80 and RL models are a\n    minority. Traditional detectors degrade individually (ConStat AUROC 0.72→0.54,\n    Min-K% 0.68→0.49) as expected from NLL gap contraction. However, DeLong test for\n    DIF vs ConStat is marginal (p=0.087) — underpowered at N=13. TOST equivalence\n    fails (p=0.11 > 0.05), so reviewers can''t rule out that DIF retention is coincidental.\n    Median-tier outcome: suggestive robustness evidence insufficient for strong claim.\n    Reviewers note ''N=13 is too few for definitive comparison; interesting pilot\n    but not convincing.'' Detection boundary at p=0.55 means DIF misses partial exploitation\n    below 55%, which is a meaningful gap.'\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: DIF vs Traditional Detector Comparison Under RL'\n    columns:\n    - Detector\n    - AUROC (SFT)\n    - AUROC (Alignment-RL)\n    - AUROC (Reasoning-RL)\n    - Δ\n    - 95% CI\n    rows:\n    - - DIF (MH)\n      - N/A (group)\n      - N/A (group)\n      - retention=0.86\n      - '-0.14'\n      - '[−0.21, −0.07]'\n    - - ConStat\n      - '0.72'\n      - '0.69'\n      - '0.54'\n      - '-0.18'\n      - '[−0.31, −0.05]'\n    - - Min-K%\n      - '0.68'\n      - '0.64'\n      - '0.49'\n      - '-0.19'\n      - '[−0.33, −0.06]'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Detection power as function of exploitation rate p ∈ [0.4, 1.0]. DIF\n    power curve shows detection boundary at p≈0.55 (TPR>0.5). Below p=0.55, DIF effect\n    size shrinks below δ=1.4 threshold. Shaded region shows ensemble coverage handoff\n    zone (p=0.4-0.6) where ConStat on non-RL models still operates.'\n  - 'Figure: Jaccard similarity of DIF flag sets across cohort compositions (SFT-only,\n    +alignment-RL, +reasoning-RL). Box plots over 100 bootstrap resamples showing\n    overlap stability.'\n  analysis: Result is directionally supportive but statistically underpowered. DIF\n    shows robustness (86% flag retention) while traditional detectors degrade, consistent\n    with theoretical prediction. However, TOST equivalence failure and marginal DeLong\n    test mean this cannot be claimed as definitive proof. In median tier, reviewers\n    acknowledge the logic but request either (a) more reasoning-RL models when available,\n    or (b) formal power analysis showing what N would be needed for conclusive evidence.\n    The detection boundary at p=0.55 is an honest limitation — partial exploitation\n    remains a blind spot. Best positioned as 'preliminary evidence consistent with\n    theoretical robustness prediction' in limitations/future work, not as a standalone\n    claim.\n- name: constat_perturbation_eval\n  plan_id: plan_003a\n  description: Independent ConStat-style perturbation testing on 250 MMLU items with\n    LLM-generated semantic reformulations across 15 models.\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_reformulated: 248\n    n_models_tested: 15\n    mean_performance_drop_original_vs_reformulated: 0.034\n    constat_detection_rate_fdr_010: 0.127\n    constat_detection_rate_fdr_005: 0.089\n    constat_auroc_vs_li_labels: 0.61\n    constat_auroc_non_rl_models: 0.67\n    constat_auroc_rl_models: 0.52\n    mean_reformulation_quality_score: 0.82\n    semantic_equivalence_human_check: 0.91\n    effect_size_detected_items_cohens_d: 0.73\n  result_rationale: ConStat operates at per-model level detecting performance drops\n    on reformulations. On non-RL models (where memorization manifests as sensitivity\n    to surface form), detection rate is moderate (12.7% at FDR=0.10). RL models show\n    minimal surface-form sensitivity (AUROC 0.52, near chance) because PPO/GRPO training\n    generalizes beyond verbatim patterns. The 3.4% mean performance drop across all\n    items is small because most items aren't contaminated — only a subset shows the\n    characteristic bimodal response pattern. AUROC vs Li et al. labels is weak (0.61)\n    because, as established, web-overlap is a mismatched ground truth for item-level\n    detection. In median tier, this provides a useful baseline comparison confirming\n    ConStat's complementary detection space (non-RL, surface-form memorization) but\n    doesn't change the paper's core narrative.\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: ConStat Detection Rates by Model Category'\n    columns:\n    - Model Category\n    - N Models\n    - Detection Rate (FDR=0.10)\n    - Mean Effect Size (d)\n    rows:\n    - - SFT-only\n      - '6'\n      - '0.153'\n      - '0.81'\n    - - Alignment-RL (DPO)\n      - '5'\n      - '0.134'\n      - '0.69'\n    - - Reasoning-RL (GRPO)\n      - '4'\n      - '0.071'\n      - '0.42'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Distribution of per-item performance drops (original − reformulated)\n    across 248 items. Bimodal distribution visible: bulk of items near zero, contaminated\n    tail at Δ>0.15.'\n  analysis: ConStat independently validates the complementary detection space claim\n    — it works where DIF doesn't (per-model, small effects) but fails where DIF succeeds\n    (RL-robust, group-differential). However, in median tier this experiment is not\n    included in the main paper because plan_003c (ensemble) was never fully executed\n    and the ensemble narrative (original Claim 2) was dropped during the pivot. ConStat\n    results go to appendix as supplementary comparison, supporting the 'detection\n    methods are complementary' theme without being a core contribution.\n- name: dvd_generation_variance_eval\n  plan_id: plan_003b\n  description: DVD generation variance detection on 12 open-weight models with 50\n    generations per item at T=0.8 on 300 MMLU items.\n  expected_results:\n    n_models_tested: 12\n    n_items_per_model: 300\n    n_generations_per_item: 50\n    temperature: 0.8\n    bimodal_detection_rate: 0.094\n    dvd_auroc_vs_dif_flags: 0.58\n    dvd_auroc_verbatim_contamination: 0.71\n    dvd_auroc_variant_contamination: 0.63\n    mean_generation_variance_clean_items: 0.34\n    mean_generation_variance_contaminated_items: 0.18\n    gpu_hours_used: 62\n    inference_failures: 3\n  result_rationale: 'DVD detects contamination via low generation variance (memorized\n    items → consistently correct). Detection rate of 9.4% reflects that most contamination\n    on MMLU is not verbatim memorization but pattern-level familiarity, which still\n    produces moderate variance. AUROC for verbatim contamination (0.71) is better\n    than variant (0.63) because verbatim produces clearer bimodal distributions. Correlation\n    with DIF flags is weak (AUROC 0.58) because DIF and DVD measure fundamentally\n    different signals: DIF measures cross-group differential, DVD measures within-model\n    consistency. In median tier, DVD provides another complementary signal but is\n    expensive (62 GPU-hours for 12 models × 300 items) and the low detection rate\n    limits practical utility. The 3 inference failures (OOM on larger models) highlight\n    scalability concerns.'\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: DVD Detection Characteristics'\n    columns:\n    - Contamination Type\n    - AUROC\n    - Detection Rate (threshold=0.10)\n    - Mean Variance Reduction\n    rows:\n    - - Verbatim\n      - '0.71'\n      - '0.134'\n      - '0.21'\n    - - Variant/Paraphrase\n      - '0.63'\n      - '0.072'\n      - '0.12'\n    - - None (clean)\n      - —\n      - 0.031 (FPR)\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Generation variance distributions for DIF-flagged vs DIF-stable items\n    across 12 models. Modest leftward shift for DIF-flagged items but heavy overlap,\n    confirming weak correspondence between group-differential (DIF) and within-model\n    (DVD) signals.'\n  analysis: DVD results are informative but not compelling enough for inclusion in\n    the pivoted paper. The weak correspondence with DIF flags (AUROC 0.58) actually\n    supports the paper's narrative that different detection methods capture different\n    contamination mechanisms. However, the high GPU cost and low detection rates make\n    this a poor cost-benefit trade for the characterization paper. In median tier,\n    this remains in supplementary material at best. The result is useful for future\n    ensemble work but doesn't strengthen any of the four core claims.\n- name: ensemble_fusion\n  plan_id: plan_003c\n  description: Attempt to fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble with\n    majority voting and weighted fusion.\n  expected_results:\n    ensemble_2of3_precision: 0.41\n    ensemble_2of3_recall: 0.23\n    ensemble_weighted_auroc: 0.66\n    ensemble_fpr: 0.087\n    individual_dif_auroc: 0.62\n    individual_constat_auroc: 0.61\n    individual_dvd_auroc: 0.58\n    cross_val_weight_variance: 0.15\n    uniform_vs_optimized_auroc_gap: 0.03\n    coverage_overlap_dif_constat: 0.31\n    coverage_overlap_dif_dvd: 0.22\n    coverage_overlap_constat_dvd: 0.38\n  result_rationale: Ensemble shows modest improvement over individuals (AUROC 0.66\n    vs best individual 0.62) but the improvement is not dramatic because the three\n    signals have limited overlap — they detect different contamination mechanisms\n    with low redundancy. 2-of-3 majority voting has good precision (0.41) but very\n    low recall (0.23) because few items trigger multiple detectors simultaneously.\n    FPR<0.10 criterion is met (0.087) but only because the ensemble is very conservative.\n    Cross-validation weight variance is high (0.15) indicating instability — different\n    folds produce different optimal weights, suggesting the calibration set is too\n    small for reliable fusion. In median tier, this experiment is effectively abandoned\n    during the pivot away from 'detection methods' framing. The ensemble was the core\n    of original Claim 2 which is now dropped.\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: Ensemble vs Individual Detector Performance'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - AUROC\n    - Precision@FPR=0.10\n    - Coverage (items flagged)\n    rows:\n    - - DIF (MH)\n      - '0.62'\n      - '0.38'\n      - 31.3%\n    - - ConStat\n      - '0.61'\n      - '0.34'\n      - 12.7%\n    - - DVD\n      - '0.58'\n      - '0.29'\n      - 9.4%\n    - - Ensemble (2-of-3)\n      - '0.66'\n      - '0.41'\n      - 6.8%\n    - - Ensemble (weighted)\n      - '0.66'\n      - '0.43'\n      - 8.2%\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Venn diagram showing overlap of flagged items across three detectors.\n    DIF flags ~3,900 items, ConStat ~380, DVD ~280. Triple overlap: 47 items. Confirms\n    complementary rather than redundant coverage.'\n  analysis: 'Ensemble fusion fails to produce a compelling unified detector because\n    the three methods target different contamination mechanisms with minimal overlap.\n    This is actually the correct scientific conclusion — it supports the pivot away\n    from ''unified contamination detector'' toward ''characterization of differential\n    item functioning.'' In median tier, plan_003c is reported as a negative result\n    in the appendix: ''ensemble approaches require contamination mechanisms to be\n    partially redundant; when mechanisms are orthogonal, fusion provides minimal lift.''\n    This negative result paradoxically supports the paper''s reframing: DIF measures\n    something fundamentally different from per-model detectors.'\n- name: bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\n  plan_id: plan_005\n  description: Fit bifactor IRT model (general + 57 subject-specific factors) to METABENCH\n    MMLU response matrix and attempt test equating via anchor items.\n  expected_results:\n    bifactor_converged: true\n    n_iterations_to_converge: 847\n    general_factor_variance_explained: 0.62\n    mean_subject_factor_variance: 0.14\n    rmsea_bifactor: 0.041\n    cfi_bifactor: 0.87\n    n_anchor_items_selected: 1842\n    anchor_stability_criterion: DIF A-class across all cohort pairs\n    equated_rho_original_vs_replaced: 0.93\n    equated_rho_anchor_only: 0.97\n    replacement_item_calibration_rmse: 0.38\n    kim_kolen_fixed_param_fit: acceptable for 68% of replacement items\n  result_rationale: Bifactor model converges but with marginal fit (CFI=0.87 < 0.90\n    threshold, RMSEA=0.041 acceptable). The 57-subject structure is too fine-grained\n    for the model to fit cleanly — general factor explains 62% of variance, individual\n    subjects contribute 14% on average, leaving ~24% residual. Anchor item selection\n    yields 1,842 stable items (14.7% of total), sufficient for equating but a relatively\n    small anchor set. Equated score correlation (ρ=0.93) falls below the target ρ≥0.95,\n    primarily because replacement item calibration via fixed-parameter approach has\n    high RMSE (0.38) — the item parameters estimated on one cohort don't transfer\n    perfectly. In median tier, this is a 'proof of concept with caveats' — equating\n    is feasible in principle but the bifactor model's marginal fit and the below-target\n    equated correlation weaken the regeneration claim. Reviewers note 'interesting\n    pilot but ρ=0.93 suggests non-trivial information loss during equating.'\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: Bifactor Model Fit Indices'\n    columns:\n    - Index\n    - Value\n    - Acceptable\n    - Good\n    rows:\n    - - RMSEA\n      - '0.041'\n      - < 0.08 ✓\n      - < 0.05 ✓\n    - - CFI\n      - '0.87'\n      - < 0.90 ✗\n      - ≥ 0.95 ✗\n    - - TLI\n      - '0.84'\n      - < 0.90 ✗\n      - ≥ 0.95 ✗\n    - - SRMR\n      - '0.063'\n      - < 0.08 ✓\n      - < 0.05 ✗\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Scree plot showing general factor dominant (eigenvalue=3,847) followed\n    by 57 subject-specific factors with eigenvalues 12-180. Clear elbow after general\n    factor confirms unidimensional dominance with multidimensional residual structure.'\n  - 'Figure: Scatter plot of equated vs original model scores (N=5,219). Correlation\n    ρ=0.93, with visible heteroscedasticity — low-ability models show more equating\n    error than high-ability models.'\n  analysis: Bifactor equating is technically feasible but falls short of production\n    quality. The marginal model fit (CFI=0.87) reflects the known challenge that 57\n    MMLU subjects create a high-dimensional latent space poorly captured by a single\n    general factor. In median tier, this experiment is reported honestly as 'pilot\n    demonstration with limitations' — it shows the path to benchmark regeneration\n    exists but requires better IRT models (multidimensional, or domain-clustered bifactor).\n    Reviewers would likely say 'interesting future direction but not mature enough\n    for a contribution claim.' This supports the paper's positioning of equating as\n    future work rather than a current claim. Plan_014 already showed within-subject\n    C%=38.1%, so the bifactor pre-check approach for reducing DIF was killed — this\n    equating pilot inherits that limitation.\n- name: item_generation_calibration\n  plan_id: plan_006\n  description: Generate replacement items for 200 DIF-flagged MMLU questions using\n    LLM generation with construct constraints, calibrate via student simulation.\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_generated: 200\n    n_items_passing_quality_filter: 156\n    difficulty_correlation_prompt_based: 0.31\n    difficulty_correlation_student_sim: 0.64\n    discrimination_correlation: 0.42\n    construct_alignment_human_rating: 0.78\n    holdout_panel_correlation: 0.58\n    mean_difficulty_mae: 0.18\n    pct_items_within_05_difficulty: 0.43\n    gpu_hours_used: 187\n  result_rationale: Item generation produces grammatically correct, construct-aligned\n    questions (78% human-rated alignment) but calibration accuracy is moderate. Student\n    simulation improves difficulty prediction (ρ=0.64) over prompt-based targeting\n    (ρ=0.31), consistent with literature (arXiv 2601.09953 reports r=0.75-0.82 — our\n    lower value reflects MMLU's broader domain coverage vs focused benchmarks). Only\n    43% of generated items fall within ±0.5 difficulty of the target, meaning majority\n    would need iterative recalibration. Discrimination correlation is lower (0.42)\n    because item discrimination depends on subtle psychometric properties harder to\n    control via generation. In median tier, this demonstrates that LLM item generation\n    is a viable but immature pipeline — insufficient for automated benchmark regeneration\n    without human-in-the-loop validation. Reviewers would flag the 58% holdout correlation\n    as 'items don't transfer well to unseen models.'\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: Calibration Method Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Difficulty ρ\n    - Discrimination ρ\n    - '% Within ±0.5 Target'\n    rows:\n    - - Prompt-based targeting\n      - '0.31'\n      - '0.28'\n      - '0.24'\n    - - Student simulation (5 students)\n      - '0.64'\n      - '0.42'\n      - '0.43'\n    - - Student simulation (20 students)\n      - '0.68'\n      - '0.45'\n      - '0.47'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Predicted vs actual item difficulty for 156 generated items. Student\n    simulation shows tighter scatter (ρ=0.64) vs prompt-based (ρ=0.31), but both show\n    ceiling/floor compression — hardest and easiest items are poorly calibrated.'\n  analysis: 'Item generation calibration shows promise but not maturity. The gap between\n    student simulation performance here (ρ=0.64) and published results (r=0.75-0.82)\n    likely reflects MMLU''s domain breadth — calibrating ''abstract algebra'' items\n    requires different student models than ''clinical knowledge.'' In median tier,\n    this supports the future work framing: ''automated benchmark regeneration is feasible\n    with improved calibration pipelines'' but is not ready as a standalone contribution.\n    Combined with plan_005''s marginal equating results, the regeneration storyline\n    (original Claim 3) remains firmly in future work territory. The 187 GPU-hours\n    for 200 items also highlights cost concerns for full-scale deployment.'\n- name: cumulative_bayesian_dif\n  plan_id: plan_007\n  description: Simulate cumulative Bayesian DIF with informative priors from prior\n    administrations over 4 successive benchmark waves.\n  expected_results:\n    single_administration_power_delta10: 0.41\n    cumulative_2_administrations_power: 0.54\n    cumulative_3_administrations_power: 0.67\n    cumulative_4_administrations_power: 0.74\n    single_administration_fpr: 0.042\n    cumulative_4_fpr: 0.051\n    power_gain_per_administration:\n    - 0.13\n    - 0.13\n    - 0.07\n    convergence_plateau: ~4 administrations\n    bayes_factor_threshold: 3.0\n    pct_items_bf_gt_3_after_4_admin: 0.68\n  result_rationale: Cumulative Bayesian approach shows expected power improvement\n    (0.41→0.74 over 4 administrations for medium effects δ=1.0) as evidence accumulates.\n    This is a well-established psychometric result (Sinharay 2006) applied to the\n    LLM benchmark setting. FPR remains controlled (0.051) because Bayesian shrinkage\n    prevents false alarms from accumulating. Power gains show diminishing returns\n    (0.13, 0.13, 0.07) — the fourth administration adds less because most detectable\n    items are already identified. In median tier, this is simulation-only (no real\n    longitudinal MMLU data exists with 4 temporal waves), so reviewers note 'theoretically\n    sound but purely simulated — would need actual longitudinal benchmark deployment\n    to validate.' Positioned as future monitoring methodology, not a current empirical\n    contribution.\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: Cumulative Power by Effect Size'\n    columns:\n    - Administrations\n    - δ=0.8\n    - δ=1.0\n    - δ=1.2\n    - δ=1.4\n    rows:\n    - - '1'\n      - '0.24'\n      - '0.41'\n      - '0.55'\n      - '0.68'\n    - - '2'\n      - '0.37'\n      - '0.54'\n      - '0.69'\n      - '0.81'\n    - - '3'\n      - '0.48'\n      - '0.67'\n      - '0.79'\n      - '0.89'\n    - - '4'\n      - '0.54'\n      - '0.74'\n      - '0.84'\n      - '0.92'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Power accumulation curves across 4 administrations, stratified by effect\n    size. Clear sigmoid shape with plateau around administration 3-4. Dashed line\n    at TPR=0.50 shows that medium effects (δ=1.0) become reliably detectable only\n    after 2+ administrations.'\n  analysis: 'Bayesian cumulative DIF is theoretically elegant and directly addresses\n    the power limitation identified in plan_001 (single-shot DIF only detects large\n    effects δ≥1.4). However, it''s purely simulation-based in the current paper because\n    METABENCH provides only a single temporal snapshot (2023 vs 2024-H1), not 4 successive\n    administrations. In median tier, reviewers appreciate the methodological contribution\n    but note ''this requires longitudinal benchmark infrastructure that doesn''t yet\n    exist.'' Best positioned as a concrete recommendation for benchmark maintainers:\n    ''if you collect response matrices over time, here''s how to accumulate evidence\n    of item degradation.'' Strengthens the ''measurement science for ML benchmarks''\n    narrative without requiring new empirical data.'\n- name: graceful_degradation_analysis\n  plan_id: plan_008\n  description: Formal analysis of benchmark validity under progressive item replacement\n    (5% to 50%) using METABENCH empirical data.\n  expected_results:\n    rho_5pct_replacement: 0.998\n    rho_10pct_replacement: 0.996\n    rho_20pct_replacement: 0.991\n    rho_50pct_replacement: 0.972\n    reliability_alpha_full: 0.943\n    reliability_alpha_50pct_replaced: 0.921\n    construct_coverage_5pct: 0.97\n    construct_coverage_50pct: 0.84\n    theoretical_bound_rho_vs_replacement_rate: ρ ≥ 1 - 1.3r (r = replacement fraction)\n    critical_replacement_threshold: ~35% before ρ < 0.98\n    dif_targeted_vs_random_replacement_rho_gap: 0.008\n  result_rationale: 'Progressive replacement analysis reveals extreme ranking robustness\n    — even 50% item replacement maintains ρ=0.972. This is consistent with plan_017''s\n    finding that ρ=0.997 after removing 31% DIF items. MMLU''s 12,508 items provide\n    massive redundancy; any subset of ~6,000+ items preserves relative model ordering.\n    Reliability (Cronbach''s α) degrades more noticeably (0.943→0.921 at 50%) because\n    replacement items have higher calibration error. Construct coverage (proportion\n    of original 57 subjects adequately sampled) drops to 0.84 at 50% because some\n    small subjects (N<50 items) lose coverage entirely. The near-linear relationship\n    ρ ≥ 1 - 1.3r holds empirically up to r=0.4. In median tier, this result cuts both\n    ways: it shows equating is feasible (replacements don''t destroy validity) but\n    also undermines urgency (if rankings are so stable, why bother replacing items?).\n    Reviewers invoke the ''so what'' critique: ρ=0.997→0.972 is still excellent.'\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: Validity Metrics Under Progressive Replacement'\n    columns:\n    - Replacement %\n    - Spearman ρ\n    - Cronbach α\n    - Construct Coverage\n    - Max Rank Δ\n    rows:\n    - - 0% (baseline)\n      - '1.000'\n      - '0.943'\n      - '1.00'\n      - '0'\n    - - 5%\n      - '0.998'\n      - '0.941'\n      - '0.97'\n      - '23'\n    - - 10%\n      - '0.996'\n      - '0.938'\n      - '0.95'\n      - '47'\n    - - 20%\n      - '0.991'\n      - '0.932'\n      - '0.91'\n      - '112'\n    - - 50%\n      - '0.972'\n      - '0.921'\n      - '0.84'\n      - '341'\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Ranking stability (ρ) vs replacement fraction. Near-linear degradation\n    with empirical bound overlaid. Shaded confidence band from 100 bootstrap resamples.\n    Vertical dashed line at 31% (observed DIF percentage) showing ρ=0.993.'\n  analysis: 'Graceful degradation analysis primarily serves as supplementary validation\n    for plan_017''s core finding. It formalizes what the ρ=0.997 result already implies:\n    MMLU has enormous measurement redundancy. In median tier, this becomes a double-edged\n    sword — reviewers use it to argue ''if the benchmark is so redundant, the 38%\n    instability finding is merely academic, not practically concerning.'' The paper''s\n    rebuttal must be: ''redundancy protects rankings but masks that 31% of items carry\n    disproportionate discriminative information (z=-8 to -49σ from plan_017). Efficiency,\n    not validity, is the real concern.'' The 35% critical threshold aligns with the\n    observed 38% DIF rate — we''re near the edge where replacement starts to matter,\n    even if we haven''t crossed it yet.'\n- name: item_focused_trees\n  plan_id: plan_009\n  description: Implement Item-Focused Trees treating model release date as continuous\n    covariate, comparing detection power and flexibility vs categorical temporal DIF.\n  expected_results:\n    n_items_analyzed: 12508\n    ift_c_pct_equivalent: 28.4\n    ift_vs_categorical_jaccard: 0.72\n    ift_unique_flags: 0.08\n    categorical_unique_flags: 0.12\n    ift_split_point_median: '2023-09-15'\n    ift_nonlinear_items_pct: 0.14\n    ift_power_gain_vs_categorical: 0.03\n    computation_time_hours: 4.2\n  result_rationale: Item-Focused Trees detect 28.4% of items as exhibiting DIF along\n    the release-date continuum (vs 31.3% for categorical temporal split). Jaccard\n    overlap of 0.72 with categorical DIF shows substantial agreement — most items\n    flagged by IFT are also flagged by categorical MH, confirming the temporal split\n    was reasonable. IFT identifies 8% unique items (gradual drift not captured by\n    binary split) and misses 12% that categorical catches (items with sharp discontinuity\n    better detected by group contrast). 14% of items show nonlinear DIF trajectories\n    (early models and late models both struggle, middle cohort performs differently).\n    Power gain is minimal (+0.03) because the binary temporal split already captures\n    the primary contrast. In median tier, IFT provides methodological sophistication\n    but minimal empirical gain — reviewers appreciate the robustness check ('arbitrary\n    temporal split didn't distort results') but note 'the continuous approach doesn't\n    change your main finding.'\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: IFT vs Categorical DIF Comparison'\n    columns:\n    - Method\n    - Items Flagged\n    - Unique Flags\n    - Overlap (Jaccard)\n    - Computation Time\n    rows:\n    - - Categorical MH (2023 vs 2024-H1)\n      - 31.3%\n      - 12%\n      - —\n      - 0.3 hours\n    - - Item-Focused Trees (continuous date)\n      - 28.4%\n      - 8%\n      - '0.72'\n      - 4.2 hours\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Example IFT splits for 4 items showing different DIF trajectories. (a)\n    Sharp discontinuity at 2023-11 (well-detected by categorical), (b) Gradual linear\n    drift (better detected by IFT), (c) U-shaped nonlinear pattern (only detected\n    by IFT), (d) No DIF (flat).'\n  analysis: 'IFT serves primarily as a robustness check confirming that the categorical\n    temporal split was not misleading. The 72% overlap validates the simpler approach\n    while the 14% nonlinear items are methodologically interesting but don''t change\n    conclusions. In median tier, this goes to supplementary material as ''sensitivity\n    analysis: continuous treatment of release date produces concordant results (Jaccard=0.72),\n    with 14% of items showing nonlinear trajectories warranting future investigation.''\n    Reviewers may note that IFT is more principled but the limited power gain doesn''t\n    justify the complexity for the main paper. Most useful as a reviewer rebuttal\n    resource: ''our arbitrary temporal split is validated by the continuous approach.'''\n- name: architecture_stratified_dif\n  plan_id: plan_010\n  description: Run DIF within decoder-only architecture subpopulation to test whether\n    IRT fit improves and spurious DIF reduces when architecture heterogeneity is removed.\n  expected_results:\n    n_decoder_only_models: 3412\n    n_ref_decoder_only_2023: 891\n    n_foc_decoder_only_2024h1: 523\n    decoder_only_c_pct: 28.2\n    full_population_c_pct: 31.3\n    delta_c_pct: -3.1\n    rmsea_full_population: 0.089\n    rmsea_decoder_only: 0.067\n    cfi_full_population: 0.78\n    cfi_decoder_only: 0.83\n    tli_decoder_only: 0.81\n    srmr_decoder_only: 0.058\n    jaccard_decoder_vs_full: 0.81\n    fpr_decoder_only: 0.282\n    tpr_decoder_only_semi_synthetic_p05: 0.79\n    delta_tpr_decoder_only: 0.508\n  result_rationale: 'Architecture stratification reduces C% from 31.3% to 28.2% —\n    a modest 3.1pp improvement, not the dramatic reduction that would indicate architecture\n    heterogeneity was the primary driver of spurious DIF. IRT fit improves marginally\n    (RMSEA 0.089→0.067, from ''poor'' to ''marginal''; CFI 0.78→0.83, still below\n    0.90 ''acceptable''). This confirms that architecture mixing contributes some\n    model misfit but is not the dominant source — the remaining 28.2% DIF reflects\n    genuine temporal item instability within decoder-only models. Jaccard overlap\n    of 0.81 with full-population flags confirms most DIF items are flagged regardless\n    of population composition. In median tier, this is the ''modest improvement''\n    outcome specified: reviewers acknowledge architecture stratification helps but\n    note ''RMSEA=0.067 and CFI=0.83 are still below standard fit thresholds, suggesting\n    deeper structural issues with unidimensional IRT on MMLU.'''\n  tables:\n  - title: 'Table: IRT Fit Comparison: Full Population vs Decoder-Only'\n    columns:\n    - Metric\n    - Full Population\n    - Decoder-Only\n    - Improvement\n    - Acceptable Threshold\n    rows:\n    - - RMSEA\n      - '0.089'\n      - '0.067'\n      - −0.022\n      - < 0.05\n    - - CFI\n      - '0.78'\n      - '0.83'\n      - '+0.05'\n      - ≥ 0.90\n    - - TLI\n      - '0.75'\n      - '0.81'\n      - '+0.06'\n      - ≥ 0.90\n    - - SRMR\n      - '0.074'\n      - '0.058'\n      - −0.016\n      - < 0.05\n    - - C% (ETS)\n      - 31.3%\n      - 28.2%\n      - −3.1pp\n      - —\n  figures_description:\n  - 'Figure: Item-level DIF effect sizes (|Δ_MH|) for decoder-only vs full population.\n    Scatter plot showing high correlation (r=0.91) with slight downward shift for\n    decoder-only, confirming most DIF persists within homogeneous architecture.'\n  - 'Figure: RMSEA distribution across 57 MMLU subjects for decoder-only population.\n    Most subjects in 0.05-0.08 range (marginal fit), with moral_scenarios and professional_law\n    as outliers (>0.10).'\n  analysis: 'Plan_010 is the proactive defense experiment against the ''IRT model\n    misfit'' reviewer concern. In median tier, the result is honest but not fully\n    reassuring: architecture stratification helps (3.1pp C% reduction, RMSEA improvement)\n    but doesn''t solve the fundamental issue that unidimensional IRT is a simplification\n    for 57-subject MMLU. The paper frames this as: ''architecture stratification provides\n    modest improvement, confirming that population homogeneity is beneficial but not\n    sufficient — multidimensional IRT or domain-specific modeling remain future directions.''\n    Reviewers likely say ''you''ve shown the problem is partially addressable but\n    haven''t solved it — reframe 38% as upper bound, 28% as lower bound under controlled\n    conditions.'' This is consistent with median-tier narrative guidance: ''instability\n    reflects a mixture'' with explicit bounds.'\n","numLines":697,"startLine":1,"totalLines":697}}}
