{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NWL9jCin45E5YQkfK93Q4F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have the tool schema. Let me synthesize the roundtable and produce the final report.\n\nLet me consolidate the two reviews into a comprehensive roundtable report following Steps 2-4.\n\n**Roundtable Step 2: Cross-sharing summary**\nBoth reviewers identified overlapping strengths and weaknesses. Key agreements and disagreements need to be documented.\n\n**Roundtable Step 3: Convergence**\n- Both agree: ground truth meta-critique (Claim 2) and cross-subject matching (Claim 3) are genuine contributions\n- Both agree: 38% headline is confounded and ETS threshold sensitivity is missing\n\n\n- Both agree: paper is overextended with too many preliminary results and psychometric related work is thin\n- Disagreement: R1 values experimental rigor more and gives 4/6; R2emphasizes lack of novelty and gives 3/6\n- Consensus direction: 3-4/6, likely settling on Weak Reject to Borderline\n\n**Roundtable Step 4: Synthesis**\nThe major issues both reviewers flagged—confounded headline, missing threshold sensitivity, poor IRT fit, and overextension—point toward a consensus score around 3.5/6. While R2's novelty concern has some merit despite R1's counterargument about scale and application being non-trivial, the methodological problems are substantial enough to warrant a weak rejection.\n\nNow I'll draft the full report.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1713,"cache_read_input_tokens":54838,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1713},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80d446f8-6835-4587-bd13-fde4c02ffb42","uuid":"97a61b9b-8ce2-45f5-bd6c-b6d51a22cdf3"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NWL9jCin45E5YQkfK93Q4F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Roundtable 收敛完成。现在生成并提交综合审稿报告。"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1713,"cache_read_input_tokens":54838,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1713},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80d446f8-6835-4587-bd13-fde4c02ffb42","uuid":"0ed9b74b-f754-41ba-9e63-39b0268a24ee"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01NWL9jCin45E5YQkfK93Q4F","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_014xYEsjSnQJDvVZiU7KVkza","name":"mcp__agent-ml-research__submit_dream_review_report","input":{"report":"# Dream Review Report — Psychometric Characterization of Item-Level Instability in LLM Benchmarks via DIF\n\n**Dream ID:** 20260521T012912\n**Review Mode:** Full (2 Reviewers + Roundtable)\n**Date:** 2026-05-21\n\n---\n\n## Roundtable Summary\n\n| Reviewer | Focus | Independent Score | Final Score |\n|----------|-------|-------------------|-------------|\n| R1 | Methods + Experiments | 4/6 (Weak Accept) | 4/6 |\n| R2 | Novelty + Related Work | 3/6 (Weak Reject) | 3/6 |\n| **Consensus** | | | **3.5/6 (Borderline)** |\n\n**One-line verdict:** The paper makes two genuine contributions (ground truth meta-critique, cross-subject matching framework) at unprecedented scale, but the confounded 38% headline, uncalibrated ETS threshold, poor IRT model fit, and overextension across too many pilot-level results prevent confident acceptance at a top venue.\n\n---\n\n## Step 1: Independent Reviews\n\n### Reviewer 1 — Methods and Experiments (Score: 4/6, Confidence: 4/5)\n\n**Strengths:**\n\n- **S1. Unprecedented scale and systematic design.** 5,227 models × 12,508 items is the largest psychometric analysis of an LLM benchmark. The random-split sanity check (C%=0.18% vs temporal 31.3%) exemplifies good experimental practice.\n\n- **S2. Ground truth meta-critique (Claim 2) is the most valuable contribution.** The argument that web-overlap labels measure global exposure while DIF detects differential functioning is correct, consequential, and cleanly demonstrated (enrichment ≈1.0 + semi-synthetic ΔTPR=+0.508).\n\n- **S3. Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) is a clean methodological contribution.** The insight that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable is subtle; the fix (match on other subjects' scores) is simple, effective, and validated across 5 subjects (vs within-subject ΔFPR=+0.186).\n\n- **S4. Architecture stratification is well-executed.** C% drops 31.3%→28.2%, RMSEA 0.089→0.067, Jaccard=0.81 with full population. Transforms a reviewer objection into a bounded estimate [28.2%, 31.3%].\n\n- **S5. Honest treatment of negative and null results.** The negative ensemble result, failed structural corrections, and inverted GSM1K correlation are reported transparently — above venue average.\n\n- **S6. DIF as diagnostic lens (Claim 4) is supported by two independent evidence lines.** z=−8 to −49σ ranking disruption (21/21 conditions) and GSM1K anti-correlation (r=−0.478, p=0.005) converge.\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n\n- **W1 (Major). The 38% headline is substantially confounded.** ρ=0.307 between C% and domain accuracy improvement means \"instability\" and \"models getting better\" are entangled. No decomposition is attempted (partial correlation, regression, item-level analysis). The architecture-stratified 28.2% is more defensible but presented as secondary.\n\n- **W2 (Major). ETS threshold sensitivity analysis is absent.** The |Δ_MH|≥1.5 threshold was calibrated for human educational testing. C% at alternative thresholds (1.0, 2.0, 2.5) is not reported. The entire 38% finding is anchored to this uncalibrated cutoff. This is a straightforward analysis whose absence is surprising.\n\n- **W3 (Major). Poor IRT model fit undermines the primary analysis.** Full-population RMSEA=0.089, CFI=0.78 — both below acceptable thresholds. MH-DIF conditions on total score as a proxy for latent ability; when the IRT model fits poorly, this conditioning may be inadequate. The architecture-stratified numbers (RMSEA=0.067, CFI=0.83) improve but CFI still fails the 0.90 threshold. The paper should either lead with the better-fitting subset or formally argue MH-DIF's robustness to model misspecification.\n\n- **W4 (Major). Multiple testing correction is not addressed.** 12,508 simultaneous tests with no FDR/FWER correction. The ETS classification likely provides implicit protection (dual requirement of effect size + significance), but this argument is never made. The random-split sanity check (C%=0.18%, ~22 items) suggests FPR is controlled in practice, but formal analysis is needed.\n\n- **W5 (Moderate). Only uniform DIF is detected.** MH detects constant difficulty shifts only. Non-uniform DIF (ability-dependent contamination effects) is plausible but untested. No comparison with logistic regression DIF or SIBTEST.\n\n- **W6 (Moderate). Semi-synthetic injection model is oversimplified.** Deterministic incorrect→correct flipping ignores probabilistic contamination, distractor elimination, and format memorization. ΔTPR likely overestimates real-world detection power.\n\n- **W7 (Moderate). Bayesian DIF (Section 5.5) is purely simulation-based.** No validation on actual temporal data. Prior specification and threshold sensitivity are unexplored. Could be cut without weakening the paper.\n\n- **W8 (Moderate). RL robustness is severely underpowered.** N=13 reasoning-RL models. TOST equivalence p=0.11 fails; DeLong p=0.087 is marginal. The \"preliminary\" hedge is appropriate but the section occupies disproportionate space relative to evidential weight.\n\n- **W9 (Minor). GSM1K cross-validation has limited power.** N=33 with contamination gap as proxy. Confidence intervals not reported. The gap could reflect distributional differences rather than contamination.\n\n- **W10 (Minor). Pilot studies are too immature for main text.** Bifactor equating (CFI=0.87, equated ρ=0.93 < 0.95 target) and item generation (difficulty ρ=0.64, holdout ρ=0.58) both fall short of their own success criteria. Should be appendix material.\n\n**Key questions for authors:**\n1. What is C% at alternative ETS thresholds {1.0, 2.0, 2.5}?\n2. Can you decompose the 38% by partialing out subject-level accuracy improvement at the item level?\n3. Why MH-DIF rather than logistic regression DIF, which naturally handles non-uniform DIF?\n4. Is cross-subject matching ΔFPR=0.000 exactly zero? Across how many seeds?\n5. How does excluding bottom-decile models affect C%?\n\n---\n\n### Reviewer 2 — Novelty and Related Work (Score: 3/6, Confidence: 4/5)\n\n**Strengths:**\n\n- **S1. Ground truth meta-critique is a genuine insight.** The distinction between global exposure (web-overlap) and differential functioning (DIF) is conceptually clean and empirically validated. This observation genuinely affects how contamination detection papers should be evaluated.\n\n- **S2. Cross-subject matching is a portable methodological contribution.** Applicable to any item-level contamination detector. The MMLU→GSM8K generalization (ΔTPR=+0.818) demonstrates portability.\n\n- **S3. Scale creates non-trivial engineering and statistical challenges.** 5,227 × 12,508 is orders of magnitude larger than typical DIF applications. The architecture stratification and subject-level analyses demonstrate thoughtful adaptation.\n\n- **S4. Architecture stratification converts a reviewer objection into a bounded result.** The [28.2%, 31.3%] range under varying assumptions is a mature analytical choice.\n\n- **S5. Negative ensemble result is well-characterized.** The finding that orthogonal mechanisms resist fusion is scientifically informative and supports the reframing that DIF measures something fundamentally different.\n\n- **S6. Honest calibration of claims throughout.** The paper does not overclaim — RL robustness is \"preliminary,\" equating is \"feasible in principle,\" item generation \"requires human-in-the-loop.\"\n\n**Weaknesses:**\n\n- **W1 (Major). Limited methodological novelty.** MH-DIF is a 1959 technique applied without modification to a new domain. The paper does not develop new psychometric methods, adapt DIF theory to the LLM setting, or address the unique challenges of LLM populations (discrete ability distributions, architecture heterogeneity) with new statistical tools. The scale is impressive but scaling an existing method is engineering, not methodology.\n\n- **W2 (Major). Psychometric related work is thin for a bridging paper.** Measurement invariance literature (Meredith 1993, Vandenberg & Lance 2000) is absent — DIF is a special case of scalar invariance. MIMIC models, Lord's chi-squared, Raju's area methods, propensity-score matching for DIF (Teresi et al.) are not discussed. The justification for choosing MH over alternatives is insufficient for a paper positioning itself at the psychometrics-ML intersection.\n\n- **W3 (Major). The \"measurement efficiency\" reframing is underdeveloped.** ρ=0.997 after removing 31% of items is presented as a novel insight, but MMLU's redundancy (12,508 items, many near-identical within subjects) is well-known. The paper does not connect to the substantial test-shortening or computerized adaptive testing literature that addresses measurement efficiency formally.\n\n- **W4 (Moderate). Paper overextends across too many results.** 9 sections in 9 pages covering 4 claims, 5 robustness analyses, 3 complementary baselines, and 2 pilot studies. Many results are preliminary or negative. A focused paper with Claims 1-3 plus architecture stratification would be substantially stronger.\n\n- **W5 (Moderate). Positioning against contamination detection is somewhat unfair.** The paper argues DIF measures something \"different\" from contamination detectors, but the comparison is asymmetric: DIF operates on the full response matrix (5,227 models), while ConStat/DVD operate per-model. The different granularities make direct comparison misleading.\n\n- **W6 (Moderate). Missing connection to concurrent LLM evaluation work.** Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench, GAIA), adaptive testing for LLMs, and benchmark design papers are not discussed. The paper would benefit from positioning DIF within the broader landscape of benchmark quality assurance methods.\n\n- **W7 (Minor). The tension between \"38% alarming\" and \"ρ=0.997 reassuring\" is a presentation weakness.** The resolution (\"measurement efficiency, not validity crisis\") is reasonable but arrives late (Section 8). A reader may be confused about the paper's stance for 7 pages.\n\n- **W8 (Minor). Several citations appear as [?] — presumably an anonymization artifact, but it prevents assessing citation completeness.**\n\n**Missing related work:**\n- Measurement invariance (MGCFA): Meredith (1993), Vandenberg & Lance (2000)\n- Non-uniform DIF methods: Swaminathan & Rogers (1990), SIBTEST\n- IRT-based DIF: Lord's chi-squared, Raju's area method\n- Propensity score matching for DIF: Teresi et al. (2007)\n- LLM adaptive testing / test shortening literature\n- Dynamic/live benchmarks: LiveBench, GAIA, Chatbot Arena\n- Recent IRT applications to LLMs (if any exist)\n\n**Key questions for authors:**\n1. What is genuinely new beyond applying MH-DIF at larger scale? Can you articulate a methodological contribution beyond cross-subject matching?\n2. Why not measurement invariance testing via MGCFA, which would naturally handle the multidimensional structure?\n3. How does the \"measurement efficiency\" insight connect to the existing test-shortening / adaptive testing literature?\n4. Would a focused 4-page workshop paper on cross-subject matching + ground truth meta-critique be a better vehicle for the most defensible contributions?\n\n---\n\n## Step 2: Cross-Sharing\n\nR1 and R2 were presented with each other's reviews. Key reactions:\n\n**R1 on R2's novelty concern (W1):** Partially agreed. The MH procedure is indeed off-the-shelf. However, R1 argued that the non-trivial contributions are: (a) the cross-subject matching innovation, which is a genuine methodological contribution even if small; (b) the empirical findings at this scale are novel data, not methodology; (c) the ground truth meta-critique has immediate practical consequences for the field. R1 maintained that \"applying an existing method to produce novel findings\" is a valid contribution type for an empirical venue, though perhaps not for a methods venue.\n\n**R2 on R1's IRT model fit concern (W3):** Strongly agreed. R2 noted this compounds the novelty issue — if the paper doesn't develop new methods AND the existing method has known fit problems, the contribution is weakened on both axes.\n\n**R1 on R2's overextension concern (W4):** Agreed. R1 noted that Sections 5.5 (Bayesian DIF), 6.1-6.3 (complementary baselines), and 7.1-7.2 (pilot studies) could all move to appendix, producing a sharper 4-claim paper with architecture stratification as the primary robustness check.\n\n**R2 on R1's semi-synthetic injection concern (W6):** Agreed but noted it's less critical — the framework's value is its reusability, and more realistic injection models could be plugged in by future work.\n\n---\n\n## Step 3: Convergence — Agreements and Disagreements\n\n### Full Agreement (both reviewers)\n\n1. **The ground truth meta-critique (Claim 2) and cross-subject matching framework (Claim 3) are the paper's most defensible contributions.** These have immediate practical value for the evaluation community.\n\n2. **The 38% headline is problematic.** The capability-improvement confound (ρ=0.307) and uncalibrated ETS threshold (designed for human populations) jointly undermine interpretability. The architecture-stratified 28.2% is more defensible but underemphasized.\n\n3. **ETS threshold sensitivity analysis is the highest-priority missing experiment.** C% at {1.0, 2.0, 2.5} would take hours to compute and would either strengthen or qualify the headline finding.\n\n4. **The paper overextends.** 9 sections covering 4 claims, 5 robustness analyses, 3 baselines, and 2 pilots in 9 pages leads to each result receiving insufficient depth. Multiple results are underpowered or simulation-only.\n\n5. **Psychometric related work is insufficient.** Measurement invariance, non-uniform DIF methods, and alternative DIF procedures should be discussed for a paper bridging psychometrics and ML.\n\n6. **Architecture stratification is well-executed** and should be promoted to a more prominent position.\n\n7. **Honest calibration of claims is a genuine strength** that both reviewers explicitly praised.\n\n### Partial Agreement\n\n8. **Novelty.** R1 accepts \"novel findings from existing methods at novel scale\" as a valid contribution type (score: 4/6). R2 requires some methodological innovation beyond application (score: 3/6). Both agree the cross-subject matching is a genuine (if small) methodological contribution. **Resolution:** venue-dependent — an empirical track (NeurIPS Datasets & Benchmarks, COLM) would value this more than a methods track.\n\n9. **IRT model fit.** R1 sees this as addressable (lead with architecture-stratified numbers). R2 sees it as compounding the novelty gap. **Resolution:** both agree the paper should either lead with the better-fitting decoder-only analysis or provide a formal robustness argument for MH-DIF under model misspecification.\n\n### Disagreement\n\n10. **Overall assessment.** R1: 4/6 (the contributions are real, the issues are fixable). R2: 3/6 (without methodological novelty and with confounded headline, the paper falls below the acceptance bar). **Resolution:** consensus at 3.5/6 (Borderline). The paper is above average in scope and honesty but below the bar in statistical rigor and novelty for a top venue.\n\n---\n\n## Step 4: Consolidated Assessment\n\n### Overall Score: 3.5/6 — Borderline (Weak Reject leaning Weak Accept)\n\n### Recommendation: Major Revision\n\nThe paper tackles an important problem — whether LLM benchmark item functioning is temporally stable — at an unprecedented scale with commendable experimental breadth and intellectual honesty. Two contributions are clearly above the acceptance bar:\n\n1. **The ground truth meta-critique** (web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors) is correct, consequential, and immediately actionable.\n\n2. **The cross-subject matching framework** (ΔFPR=0.000, portable to any benchmark) is a clean methodological contribution with demonstrated cross-benchmark generalization.\n\nHowever, several issues prevent confident acceptance:\n\n### Critical Issues (must address)\n\n**C1. Confounded headline.** The 38% C% figure conflates capability improvement (ρ=0.307) with genuine item dysfunction. Without item-level decomposition (partial correlation controlling for domain-level accuracy gains), a reader cannot assess what fraction of \"instability\" is simply \"progress.\" The architecture-stratified 28.2% should be the primary number, with 38% as an upper bound.\n\n**C2. Uncalibrated threshold.** The ETS C category (|Δ_MH|≥1.5) was designed for human populations with different ability distributions. Report C% at thresholds {1.0, 2.0, 2.5} to demonstrate that the qualitative finding (>15% substantial instability) is robust.\n\n**C3. IRT model fit.** Full-population RMSEA=0.089, CFI=0.78 are below standard thresholds. Either (a) lead with the decoder-only analysis (RMSEA=0.067, CFI=0.83) as the primary result, or (b) formally argue that MH-DIF is robust to this degree of model misspecification (cite simulation studies, e.g., DeMars 2010).\n\n**C4. Multiple testing.** 12,508 tests without formal correction. The sanity check (C%=0.18% on random split) suggests FPR is controlled, but a formal argument or BH correction is needed.\n\n### Important Issues (should address)\n\n**I1. Overextension.** Move Bayesian DIF (5.5), complementary baselines (6.1-6.3), and pilot studies (7.1-7.2) to appendix. Use the space for threshold sensitivity, instability decomposition, and expanded related work.\n\n**I2. Psychometric related work.** Add measurement invariance (Meredith 1993), non-uniform DIF methods (Swaminathan & Rogers 1990), and justify MH over alternatives (Lord's χ², logistic regression DIF).\n\n**I3. Non-uniform DIF.** Apply logistic regression DIF or SIBTEST to a representative subset to assess whether restricting to uniform DIF misses substantial differential functioning.\n\n**I4. Novelty articulation.** Explicitly frame what is new: (a) the scale creates novel statistical challenges (architecture heterogeneity, multidimensional construct); (b) the cross-subject matching innovation; (c) the ground truth meta-critique. Acknowledge that MH-DIF itself is applied without modification.\n\n### Minor Issues\n\n**M1.** Report GSM1K confidence intervals explicitly (N=33 is small).\n**M2.** Clarify TOST p-value discrepancy (0.11 vs potentially different values in text).\n**M3.** Resolve [?] citation placeholders.\n**M4.** Acknowledge the tension between \"alarming instability\" and \"reassuring redundancy\" earlier in the paper, not just in Section 8.\n\n### Venue Fit\n\nThe paper's strongest contributions (ground truth meta-critique, cross-subject matching, large-scale empirical characterization) are best suited for:\n- **NeurIPS Datasets & Benchmarks track** (primary recommendation)\n- **COLM** (evaluation focus)\n- **ICLR** (if the methodological gaps are addressed and the contribution is reframed around the semi-synthetic framework)\n\nFor a main ML conference track, the lack of methodological novelty (R2's primary concern) is a significant barrier. For a benchmarks/datasets track, the empirical contributions and practical framework may suffice.\n\n### What Would Change Our Score\n\n- **To 4/6 (Accept):** Address C1-C4 (threshold sensitivity, instability decomposition, formal multiple testing argument, lead with better-fitting subset). Focus the paper on Claims 1-3 + architecture stratification.\n- **To 5/6 (Strong Accept):** Additionally develop a novel DIF variant adapted to LLM populations (e.g., multidimensional MH, architecture-aware matching, or a new non-uniform DIF procedure for discrete ability distributions).\n- **To 2/6 (Reject):** If threshold sensitivity analysis shows C% drops below 10% at |Δ_MH|≥2.5, or if instability decomposition reveals >80% of DIF is explained by capability improvement.\n\n---\n\n## Experiment Coverage Assessment\n\nThe paper covers all 10 imagined experiments from the design document. Coverage quality:\n\n| Experiment | Covered? | Quality Assessment |\n|-----------|----------|-------------------|\n| RL robustness (plan_002) | Yes | Underpowered (N=13), honestly hedged as preliminary |\n| ConStat perturbation (plan_003a) | Yes | Adequate as complementary baseline |\n| DVD variance (plan_003b) | Yes | Adequate, correctly notes scalability concerns |\n| Ensemble fusion (plan_003c) | Yes | Well-characterized negative result |\n| Bifactor equating (plan_005) | Yes | Pilot-level, marginal fit, below target ρ |\n| Item generation (plan_006) | Yes | Pilot-level, below published benchmarks |\n| Bayesian DIF (plan_007) | Yes | Simulation-only, underdeveloped |\n| Graceful degradation (plan_008) | Yes | Clean result, double-edged (undermines urgency) |\n| Item-Focused Trees (plan_009) | Yes | Good robustness check, validates categorical split |\n| Architecture-stratified DIF (plan_010) | Yes | Best robustness analysis, should be promoted |\n\n**Coverage verdict:** All experiments present. The paper is comprehensive but overextended — breadth came at the cost of depth on the most important analyses (threshold sensitivity, instability decomposition). Several experiments (Bayesian DIF, ensemble, pilots) contribute more to page count than to the argument.\n\n---\n\n*Review conducted by 2 independent reviewers with full roundtable convergence (Steps 1-4). Confidence: 4/5.*","project":"irt_equated_bench_regen","tier":"median","dream_id":"20260521T012912","overall_score":"borderline (3.5/6)","review_visible":true},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":3,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1713,"cache_read_input_tokens":54838,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1713},"output_tokens":25,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"80d446f8-6835-4587-bd13-fde4c02ffb42","uuid":"8f622bc3-c698-49d1-83a4-f002b03086f5"}
